WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.000
Cloud incidents can involve virtual machines, containers, serverless functions,

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:11.220
managed services, object storage, snapshots, firewall rules, security groups, routing,

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:12.880
and data flows.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.940
The response approach depends on the affected service and evidence available.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:22.360
A compromised virtual machine offers different evidence and containment options than an

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.940
exposed storage bucket or suspicious serverless function update.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.400
Workload response should preserve evidence before destructive actions when practical.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:36.060
Snapshotting disks, capturing metadata, recording configuration, preserving relevant logs,

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:41.000
and documenting network controls can allow later analysis while containment and recovery

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:42.100
proceed.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.600
Quarantine patterns may include restricting network access,

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:50.140
moving a workload into an isolated security group, detaching risky permissions,

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.320
or replacing the workload from trusted infrastructure-as-code.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:57.220
Storage incidents often involve exposure or unexpected access.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:01.600
Exposed object storage, public snapshots, broadly shared files,

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.700
or unexpected data transfer require careful scoping.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:09.080
Responders need to identify what data was accessible, when exposure began,

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:14.740
who or what accessed it, whether logs are available, and what business, privacy, legal,

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.520
or customer notification questions may follow.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:23.340
Network incidents may appear as suspicious outbound traffic, unexpected firewall changes,

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:27.400
exposed management ports, or unusual connectivity between services.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:31.780
Cloud network controls are highly programmable, so configuration history matters.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.040
A rule may have been changed manually, through automation,

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.580
or as part of an infrastructure deployment.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.680
Understanding the change path helps prevent recurrence.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:45.780
Cloud-native containment should avoid destroying evidence or creating unnecessary outages.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.400
Sometimes isolation is better than termination.

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:52.620
Sometimes rebuilding from known-good infrastructure is safer than trying to clean a

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:54.040
running workload.

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:59.380
The right choice depends on business criticality, evidence needs, data sensitivity,

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.800
and the confidence responders have in the affected resource.
